1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,926
Night.

2
00:00:00,926 --> 00:00:02,583
The Arabian Sea.

3
00:00:02,583 --> 00:00:07,442
Somewhere under these waves, an Indian submarine is running out of battery.

4
00:00:07,442 --> 00:00:09,734
Imagine you're in its control room.

5
00:00:09,734 --> 00:00:11,512
Nobody raises a voice.

6
00:00:11,512 --> 00:00:14,143
The captain gives one quiet order.

7
00:00:14,143 --> 00:00:15,556
Raise the snorkel.

8
00:00:15,556 --> 00:00:18,813
A thin steel mast rises through black water.

9
00:00:18,813 --> 00:00:20,937
The diesel engines roar to life.

10
00:00:20,937 --> 00:00:26,437
And for the next few hours, you are easier to find than at almost any other time on your patrol.

11
00:00:26,437 --> 00:00:29,168
You're inside a machine built to disappear.

12
00:00:29,168 --> 00:00:32,039
This is the most dangerous part of its life.

13
00:00:32,039 --> 00:00:34,334
And it has to do it every few days.

14
00:00:34,334 --> 00:00:38,580
So why would anyone build a hiding machine that has to show itself?

15
00:00:38,580 --> 00:00:39,797
There is a cure.

16
00:00:39,797 --> 00:00:41,460
You've probably heard its name.

17
00:00:41,460 --> 00:00:44,617
A-I-P, air independent propulsion.

18
00:00:44,617 --> 00:00:47,169
Online, they call it the ghost maker.

19
00:00:47,169 --> 00:00:50,759
Our submarines will become invisible, the headlines say.

20
00:00:50,759 --> 00:00:51,960
But here's the catch.

21
00:00:51,960 --> 00:00:56,279
To fit it, India has to cut one of its own submarines in two.

22
00:00:56,279 --> 00:00:57,721
And it gets stranger.

23
00:00:57,721 --> 00:01:00,917
India built this machine, with its own scientists.

24
00:01:00,917 --> 00:01:02,547
It has run on land.

25
00:01:02,547 --> 00:01:06,918
Yet the Navy leaned towards a German design for its next submarines.

26
00:01:06,918 --> 00:01:08,579
So was the Navy wrong?

27
00:01:08,579 --> 00:01:11,122
Or was India's machine not good enough?

28
00:01:11,122 --> 00:01:13,196
The answer is, both were right.

29
00:01:13,196 --> 00:01:15,629
By the end of this film, you'll see why.

30
00:01:15,629 --> 00:01:18,570
Start with the people whose job is to find you.

31
00:01:18,570 --> 00:01:20,084
Now switch sides.

32
00:01:20,084 --> 00:01:21,160
You're the hunter.

33
00:01:21,160 --> 00:01:25,698
You're flying a P-Eight-I of the Indian Navy, hours out over the sea.

34
00:01:25,698 --> 00:01:27,828
You don't look for the submarine itself.

35
00:01:27,828 --> 00:01:29,997
You look for what it leaves behind.

36
00:01:29,997 --> 00:01:31,024
A mast.

37
00:01:31,024 --> 00:01:33,722
The thin white wake it draws on the water.

38
00:01:33,722 --> 00:01:35,310
A dot on your radar.

39
00:01:35,310 --> 00:01:37,217
The noise of a diesel engine.

40
00:01:38,109 --> 00:01:39,300
Now switch back.

41
00:01:39,300 --> 00:01:40,958
You're in the submarine again.

42
00:01:40,958 --> 00:01:43,749
So why would you ever raise that mast?

43
00:01:43,749 --> 00:01:49,193
Because under water, everything you depend on runs on one giant battery.

44
00:01:49,193 --> 00:01:53,062
The motor, the lights, the air plant, the computers.

45
00:01:53,062 --> 00:01:56,597
And the engines that charge your battery need air.

46
00:01:56,597 --> 00:01:58,440
Down there, there is none.

47
00:01:58,440 --> 00:02:00,832
So how long can your battery last?

48
00:02:00,832 --> 00:02:01,957
A few days.

49
00:02:01,957 --> 00:02:07,670
The makers of India's Kalvari class say about four, and only if you move at a crawl.

50
00:02:07,670 --> 00:02:12,969
Then you must come up near the surface, raise your mast, and breathe for hours.

51
00:02:12,969 --> 00:02:15,473
Picture your patrol as a strip of time.

52
00:02:15,473 --> 00:02:17,684
Every few days, a red mark.

53
00:02:17,684 --> 00:02:22,749
Each red mark is a few hours when you can be heard, seen, or caught on radar.

54
00:02:22,749 --> 00:02:25,750
For a submarine navy, that's the whole game, right?

55
00:02:25,750 --> 00:02:26,889
Fewer red marks.

56
00:02:26,889 --> 00:02:28,327
Shorter red marks.

57
00:02:28,327 --> 00:02:30,720
That's exactly what A-I-P does.

58
00:02:30,720 --> 00:02:34,143
It's a second engine that doesn't need air from outside.

59
00:02:34,143 --> 00:02:36,267
Three main kinds are in use today.

60
00:02:36,267 --> 00:02:40,063
A Stirling engine, a steam turbine, and a fuel cell.

61
00:02:40,063 --> 00:02:41,912
India chose the fuel cell.

62
00:02:41,912 --> 00:02:44,713
And for you, the difference is huge.

63
00:02:44,713 --> 00:02:49,066
About four days on the battery becomes about two weeks under water.

64
00:02:49,066 --> 00:02:52,409
That's D-R-D-O's own figure from its land trials.

65
00:02:52,409 --> 00:02:53,880
So, a ghost?

66
00:02:53,880 --> 00:02:54,785
Not quite.

67
00:02:54,785 --> 00:02:57,053
A-I-P lets you crawl for weeks.

68
00:02:57,053 --> 00:02:58,613
It doesn't let you run.

69
00:02:58,613 --> 00:03:02,425
Push for speed, and you're back on the battery, draining it fast.

70
00:03:02,425 --> 00:03:05,728
And even when you're quiet, you can still be found.

71
00:03:05,728 --> 00:03:08,593
Active sonar can bounce sound off your hull.

72
00:03:08,593 --> 00:03:11,752
And the moment you raise a mast, you can still be seen.

73
00:03:12,673 --> 00:03:15,897
What it really buys you is fewer red marks.

74
00:03:15,897 --> 00:03:18,304
Fewer moments of being exposed.

75
00:03:18,304 --> 00:03:21,561
At sea, that can decide whether you come home.

76
00:03:21,561 --> 00:03:25,183
Then you might ask, why not just build nuclear submarines?

77
00:03:25,183 --> 00:03:26,656
India does build them.

78
00:03:26,656 --> 00:03:30,274
But they are far bigger, far more costly, and few.

79
00:03:30,274 --> 00:03:36,252
In the shallow waters close to our coast, a small, quiet diesel boat is still the right tool.

80
00:03:36,252 --> 00:03:38,680
So India built its own A-I-P.

81
00:03:38,680 --> 00:03:40,707
And it built it in its own way.

82
00:03:40,707 --> 00:03:45,549
Germany's fuel cell submarines carry their hydrogen locked inside metal tanks.

83
00:03:45,549 --> 00:03:48,468
India's carries no hydrogen gas at all.

84
00:03:48,468 --> 00:03:51,476
Instead, it carries a dry white powder.

85
00:03:51,476 --> 00:03:53,236
Sodium boro-hydride.

86
00:03:53,236 --> 00:03:56,002
Add water, and the powder gives off hydrogen.

87
00:03:56,002 --> 00:03:59,824
Right there, inside the submarine, only as much as you need.

88
00:03:59,824 --> 00:04:02,136
How much power is hiding in that powder?

89
00:04:02,136 --> 00:04:07,140
One spoonful, about seven grams, makes enough electricity to charge your phone.

90
00:04:07,140 --> 00:04:09,630
That's our rough calculation, on paper.

91
00:04:09,630 --> 00:04:11,474
Now picture tonnes of it.

92
00:04:11,474 --> 00:04:13,723
The hydrogen then meets oxygen.

93
00:04:13,723 --> 00:04:17,838
The oxygen is carried as a liquid, kept very, very cold.

94
00:04:17,838 --> 00:04:22,019
The two meet inside a fuel cell, and its heart is an acid.

95
00:04:22,019 --> 00:04:23,322
You won't see a flame.

96
00:04:23,322 --> 00:04:24,512
No explosion.

97
00:04:24,512 --> 00:04:26,901
Just a steady flow of electricity.

98
00:04:26,901 --> 00:04:28,670
And one more thing comes out.

99
00:04:28,670 --> 00:04:29,374
Water.

100
00:04:29,374 --> 00:04:33,175
That water flows back to the powder, to make more hydrogen.

101
00:04:33,175 --> 00:04:36,911
Would you want all this inside a sealed steel tube with you?

102
00:04:36,911 --> 00:04:40,990
In October twenty twenty-six, India's Defence Secretary said it simply.

103
00:04:40,990 --> 00:04:46,207
These systems need a strict safety culture, with no compromise.

104
00:04:46,207 --> 00:04:49,432
On paper, India's design has a real edge.

105
00:04:49,432 --> 00:04:52,466
There's no tank of hydrogen gas waiting to leak.

106
00:04:52,466 --> 00:04:53,474
And the price?

107
00:04:53,474 --> 00:04:58,651
Weight, and a salty waste liquid that the boat must hold, or pump out to sea.

108
00:04:58,651 --> 00:04:59,613
Powder in.

109
00:04:59,613 --> 00:05:00,535
Power out.

110
00:05:00,535 --> 00:05:02,736
Under water, for weeks.

111
00:05:02,736 --> 00:05:04,438
But there's a problem.

112
00:05:04,438 --> 00:05:08,098
A Kalvari submarine is about sixty-seven metres long.

113
00:05:08,098 --> 00:05:11,868
Walk through it, and you'll find every metre already full.

114
00:05:11,868 --> 00:05:14,330
So where does this whole plant go?

115
00:05:14,330 --> 00:05:16,587
You open the submarine like a book.

116
00:05:16,587 --> 00:05:19,944
At Mazagon Dock in Mumbai, the hull is cut in two.

117
00:05:19,944 --> 00:05:25,854
A brand-new section is built, holding the powder, the oxygen, the fuel cells and the waste.

118
00:05:25,854 --> 00:05:29,293
It slides into the gap, and the two parts are joined around it.

119
00:05:29,293 --> 00:05:32,740
Reports say the boat comes out about ten metres longer.

120
00:05:32,740 --> 00:05:34,467
Picture ten metres.

121
00:05:34,467 --> 00:05:37,788
That's about four autorickshaws, parked bumper to bumper.

122
00:05:37,788 --> 00:05:40,756
Now, would you trust a hull that has been cut open?

123
00:05:40,756 --> 00:05:48,144
That's why the submarine's original designer, France's Naval Group, watches over the work and signs off the design for safe fitting.

124
00:05:48,144 --> 00:05:50,789
So the plan is ready, and the machine works.

125
00:05:50,789 --> 00:05:52,400
That's what you'd expect, right?

126
00:05:52,400 --> 00:05:53,977
Here's the honest answer.

127
00:05:53,977 --> 00:05:55,382
Does it work on land?

128
00:05:55,382 --> 00:05:55,992
Yes.

129
00:05:55,992 --> 00:06:03,979
On the eighth of March, twenty twenty-one, D-R-D-O's land prototype was proven, in its endurance mode and its maximum power mode.

130
00:06:03,979 --> 00:06:06,080
But it has never been to sea.

131
00:06:06,978 --> 00:06:09,918
And at sea, the rules are different.

132
00:06:09,918 --> 00:06:16,290
For its next six submarines, the Navy asked for an air independent engine already proven at sea.

133
00:06:16,290 --> 00:06:17,368
Not on shore.

134
00:06:17,368 --> 00:06:18,354
At sea.

135
00:06:18,354 --> 00:06:24,757
Reports say a bid from L and T with Spain's Navantia was turned down for exactly this reason.

136
00:06:24,757 --> 00:06:28,096
Its system had been shown on shore, not at sea.

137
00:06:28,096 --> 00:06:31,896
Germany's fuel cell boats have been at sea for about twenty years.

138
00:06:31,896 --> 00:06:34,743
So the Navy leaned towards a German design.

139
00:06:34,743 --> 00:06:37,091
Does that mean India's machine is bad?

140
00:06:37,091 --> 00:06:37,733
No.

141
00:06:37,733 --> 00:06:41,171
It means nobody has proven our system at sea yet.

142
00:06:41,171 --> 00:06:44,341
Look at it from both sides, and the trap becomes clear.

143
00:06:44,341 --> 00:06:48,903
The Navy can't bet a war submarine on an engine that has never been to sea.

144
00:06:48,903 --> 00:06:53,267
And the engine can't go to sea until a submarine is cut open for it.

145
00:06:53,267 --> 00:06:54,413
Both are right.

146
00:06:54,413 --> 00:06:55,992
That's what makes it a trap.

147
00:06:55,992 --> 00:06:58,189
And there's one more lock on that trap.

148
00:06:58,189 --> 00:07:04,125
A submarine can only be cut open in a big refit, when it leaves service for months of repair.

149
00:07:04,125 --> 00:07:06,861
And that comes once in several years.

150
00:07:06,861 --> 00:07:09,151
So what happened in twenty twenty-five?

151
00:07:09,151 --> 00:07:12,351
India's first Kalvari boat was in for its refit.

152
00:07:12,351 --> 00:07:14,303
The new engine wasn't ready.

153
00:07:14,303 --> 00:07:16,306
The boat came out without it.

154
00:07:16,306 --> 00:07:18,038
The window closed.

155
00:07:18,038 --> 00:07:21,326
So if you were in charge, how would you break the trap?

156
00:07:21,326 --> 00:07:23,382
You'd start with one submarine.

157
00:07:23,382 --> 00:07:25,960
Reports say it will be I-N-S Khanderi.

158
00:07:26,810 --> 00:07:34,226
This October, the heart of this machine started its next round of tests, at L and T's yard in Hazira, Gujarat.

159
00:07:34,226 --> 00:07:38,136
Next, it gets powered up and tested to the Navy's rules.

160
00:07:38,136 --> 00:07:40,831
Early next year, it goes to Mazagon Dock.

161
00:07:40,831 --> 00:07:46,714
And the Defence Secretary says the fitting comes around the middle of next year, with sea trials to follow.

162
00:07:46,714 --> 00:07:49,835
Designed at D-R-D-O's lab in Ambernath.

163
00:07:49,835 --> 00:07:51,546
Built by L and T.

164
00:07:51,546 --> 00:07:53,390
Fitted by Mazagon Dock.

165
00:07:53,390 --> 00:07:58,470
And the plan is to give it to all six Kalvari boats, one refit at a time.

166
00:07:58,470 --> 00:08:00,767
Meanwhile, is the sea waiting?

167
00:08:00,767 --> 00:08:01,525
No.

168
00:08:01,525 --> 00:08:05,307
China's Yuan class submarines already have this technology.

169
00:08:05,307 --> 00:08:13,161
In April twenty twenty-six, Pakistan commissioned its first Hangor class submarine, which its navy says has A-I-P too.

170
00:08:13,161 --> 00:08:18,217
So everything you've heard now rests on one boat, and one set of trials.

171
00:08:18,217 --> 00:08:22,592
If those trials work, India will have what no tender can turn down.

172
00:08:22,592 --> 00:08:25,543
Its own A-I-P, proven at sea.

173
00:08:25,543 --> 00:08:29,807
The trap opens, for this boat and every boat after it.

174
00:08:29,807 --> 00:08:32,624
Now go back to that night in the Arabian Sea.

175
00:08:32,624 --> 00:08:34,292
You're in the control room again.

176
00:08:34,292 --> 00:08:35,575
The battery is low.

177
00:08:35,575 --> 00:08:37,104
You wait for the order.

178
00:08:37,104 --> 00:08:39,980
And this time, the mast stays down.

179
00:08:39,980 --> 00:08:43,312
The submarine slips deeper, and keeps going.

180
00:08:43,312 --> 00:08:45,636
But for now, that's only a hope.

181
00:08:45,636 --> 00:08:48,693
If the trials fail, the trap closes again.

182
00:08:48,693 --> 00:08:52,703
That's why the coming year matters more than any headline you'll read.

183
00:08:52,703 --> 00:08:54,841
Next, we go even deeper.

184
00:08:54,841 --> 00:09:01,466
Six thousand metres down, inside a titanium sphere built to carry Indians to the bottom of the ocean.

